comments.body:
@participant16 @participant4  Thanks for the feedback.

When we first moved to Discussions, there was a limit of 10 categories so I didn't create board-specific categories. This has subsequently been increased to 25. If I understand both of your feedback, having more, focused categories (e.g. "ESP32") would help a lot. I'm more than happy to add more categories (a few people have mentioned this now) so I guess we'll start with one for each of the main ports.

I also understand that a "subscribe to category" feature is in the works, as well as a way to group categories into sub-categories. You can see on the "GitHub Community" (i.e. run by GitHub) discussions page, they already have the sub-category feature enabled -- https://github.com/community/community/discussions/categories/discussions

@participant16 I think you mentioned the "Welcome" banner also on Discord. This is actually supposed to be the pinned post (it makes more sense when there is more than one!). Hopefully soon there'll be a v1.20 release announcement to pin next to it :). I agree that it does take a lot of vertical space though. I've already sent feedback about the "organisation discussions" banner too.

If you have UX feedback for discussions, then the GitHub team are quite responsive to feedback on their discussions page linked above.

@participant16 @participant4 I have added categories for each of the major ports. See announcement https://github.com/orgs/micropython/discussions/10189

It's a long list now, but hopefully we can group them at some point in the future. Looks like people have already started using the ESP32 category: https://github.com/micropython/micropython/discussions/categories/ports-esp32

> @reporter I think that If you even no have the MicroPython ports as categories, to have specific boards as categories will me a mess - because there are so many boards for each MicroPython port.

@participant4 Yep, of course I meant port-specific categories not board-specific (!!!), this was a typo (I said ports later in the comment). This is exactly what is now implemented.
